    "description": {
      "type": "doc",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "Ha Long Bay lies in the Gulf of Tonkin in northeast Vietnam, about 170 kilometres east of Hanoi. Close to 2,000 limestone islands and pillars rise from the water, many hollowed by caves and ringed by small beaches. Lan Ha Bay and Cat Ba Island continue the karst landscape to the south.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "The name means descending dragon, after a legend of dragons that spat out jewels to form the islands. UNESCO listed the bay in 1994. Fishing families once lived in floating villages like Cua Van, and a few rafts and fish farms remain. Grilled squid cakes, cha muc, are the local dish.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "March to May and September to November bring calm water and clear views. Summer brings storms that can cancel sailings. Book an overnight cruise to see the bay at dawn, before the day boats arrive.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
